\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Distribution of integer participation counts $N_{\mathrm  {corr}}= F_{\mathrm  {m}}^{\ast }/f_{\mathrm  {base},k}$ in the real data (blue) compared with the shuffle null (orange). Both exhibit broad support up to $N_{\mathrm  {corr}}\sim 40$, with clusters near low integers and around $N_{\mathrm  {corr}}\simeq 24$, corresponding to families whose base frequency lies near unity. }}{3}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:N_hist}{{1}{3}{Distribution of integer participation counts $\Ncorr = \Fmstar /\fbasefam {k}$ in the real data (blue) compared with the shuffle null (orange). Both exhibit broad support up to $\Ncorr \sim 40$, with clusters near low integers and around $\Ncorr \simeq 24$, corresponding to families whose base frequency lies near unity}{figure.1}{}}
